WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.017
Now we combine several tables in one query.

00:00:03.017 --> 00:00:08.847
The first join moves from patients to admissions using patient_id.

00:00:08.847 --> 00:00:15.692
Because the result is at admission detail, using INNER JOIN is reasonable if we only care about admissions

00:00:15.692 --> 00:00:17.712
that belong to valid patients.

00:00:17.712 --> 00:00:24.407
The second join moves from each admission to the attending staff member using attending_staff_id.

00:00:24.407 --> 00:00:30.167
Here we use LEFT JOIN so the admission can remain even if the staff reference is missing.

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:33.162
Notice the path: patient to admission, then admission to staff.

00:00:33.162 --> 00:00:41.187
That path matters. We are not joining tables just because they have columns that look similar.

00:00:41.187 --> 00:00:44.037
We are following documented relationships.

00:00:44.037 --> 00:00:48.377
This is exactly how larger real-world queries should be designed.

00:00:48.377 --> 00:00:52.882
For example, in retail the path might be customer to order, then order to payment.

00:00:52.882 --> 00:00:58.527
In a university it might be student to registration, then registration to course.

00:00:58.527 --> 00:01:02.752
Write the relationship path before writing the SQL.

00:01:02.752 --> 00:01:04.322
Also keep checking the grain.

00:01:04.322 --> 00:01:12.442
Adding the staff table should not create more admission rows if each attending staff ID points to at most one

00:01:12.442 --> 00:01:19.360
staff member. If the row count increases unexpectedly, that is a signal to investigate the relationship.
